    "title": "7-Eleven plans to close 645 stores across North America",
    "text": "The closures mark the fifth straight year the chain has closed more locations than it opened in North America. 7-Eleven plans to close 645 convenience stores in North America over the next fiscal year — the most aggressive round of cutbacks yet for a chain that has been shrinking its footprint for half a decade. Seven & i Holdings, the chain's Japanese parent company, also recently pushed back a highly anticipated stock market debut until at least 2027. The announcements were both made as part of the company's earnings release on April 9. According to Seven & i's earnings documents, 7-Eleven, Inc., the U.S. arm of the chain based in Irving, Texas, expects to close 645 retail locations in its current fiscal year while opening just 205 — a net loss of 440 stores. Some of those locations will not disappear entirely: the parent company said some closures will happen because of conversion to wholesale fuel stores, a model that keeps a location operating as a gas supplier without the retail staff and overhead of a full convenience store. The closures would make fiscal year 2026 the fifth year in a row that the company has closed more stores than it opened. 7-Eleven did not release a list of stores it is planning to close. The chain already had a tough stretch in recent years. 7-Eleven closed 373 North American stores in fiscal 2025 — and 474 the year before that. Even as it closes hundreds of locations, 7-Eleven plans to invest heavily in a new format: larger stores with expanded food menus, seating areas and, in many cases, fuel stations. The chain has set a goal of opening 1,300 new-format North American stores by 2030. The strategy mirrors a broader shift across the convenience store industry, where chains like Wawa and Casey's General Stores have built loyal followings — and strong sales — by competing more aggressively on fresh, made-to-order food. The 7-Eleven chain was initially founded in Dallas in 1927 as the Southland Ice Co. As it expanded its volume of convenience stores in the following years, the company was rebranded as Tote'm Stores, until 1946, when its name was changed to 7-Eleven to reflect its then-companywide store operating hours of 7 a.m. to 11 p.m.",
